From 9b909b8448015c7018e0d55519b39601e2a2885e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 01:28:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/15] Add Cal.com harvest and Cold Open send-platform
 definitional facts
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

harvestCalCom(engagementId, apiKey) hits GET /v2/me — the exact endpoint
this app's own CalComClient.checkCredentialHealth already calls (same
base URL, same cal-api-v2-key header), just parsing the body for the
first time. Response shape ({status, data: {...}}) and field names
(username, email, name, timeZone) confirmed against Cal.com's own
official OpenAPI spec (github.com/calcom/cal.com, docs/api-reference/v2/
openapi.json), fetched directly this session — same confidence tier as
Mailchimp/GHL/Twilio.

harvestColdOpenSendPlatformChoice covers cold_open_instantly/smartlead/
lemlist/reply_io: no account-metadata endpoint needed, since connecting
any of them at all IS choosing it as sendPlatform — same definitional
pattern account-harvest.ts already uses for bookingPlatform/emailPlatform.
Writes the real JSONB shape (schema.ts's sendPlatform: {platform,
baseUrl?}), not a bare string a future resolver would have to re-wrap.
The provider->platform-id translation (cold_open_instantly -> instantly)
is checked directly against schema.ts's ColdOpenSendPlatformId, not
guessed.

This turn's research (checking credential-test-providers.ts, this app's
own real provider list, before searching externally) also resolved
several open items from earlier without writing any code for them:
- Webflow, WordPress, Vercel, Slack have NO real credential connection in
  this app despite being schema enum values — confirmed by their absence
  from credential-test-providers.ts's exhaustive, hand-verified list.
  Building harvesters for any of them would have been dead code, the
  same near-miss this session already caught once with GHL.
- Apollo and PDL are deliberately excluded from that same list because
  their only calls are paid, per-lookup, billed against the buyer's
  account — the identical reasoning means a "harvest account facts" call
  for either would carry the same silent-cost risk this app's own code
  already refuses to introduce elsewhere. Not built, for the same reason.
- Apify's leadSourceType and the 4 send platforms' campaignMap are real,
  confirmed-reachable data (this app already calls their /campaigns
  endpoints for credential health checks) but don't fit today's
  "one silent fact on connect" pattern — leadSources is an array needing
  more shape than a definitional write, and campaignMap needs a picker
  UI, not a silent suggestion. Left for a later, UI-aware pass.

Verified: typecheck clean, zero new lint issues, pre-existing test
failure count unchanged.
